  "app.version_privacy_policy_how_to": "<p class=\"appver\"><b>{0}</b> version {1} ({2}) · <a href=\"#\" class=\"wr-priv-link\">Privacy Policy</a></p><h3>How to use</h3><ol><li>Choose the target type at the top.</li><li><b>Switching features</b> (Pro): by default there's an <b>icon bar</b> at the bottom (Call·Check, SIUS slip, Analysis, Records, Settings) that fades out after 3 seconds idle; a light swipe outside the target (or up from the very bottom) brings it back. In the “Settings” sub-tab, under “Interface”, you can switch to <b>B floating button</b> (round button at the bottom right) or <b>C swipe left/right</b> (swipe outside the target).</li><li><b>Settings</b>: split into “Settings” and “Help” sub-tabs; the app remembers the one you viewed last. With C swipe, swiping on this page first switches sub-tabs; swiping right again from “Settings” goes back to “SIUS slip”.</li><li><b>Camera buttons on the Analysis page</b> (Gallery / Next photo / File) and the <b>three SIUS-slip babies</b> (Camera / Gallery / File): after 1.8 seconds idle they slide to the screen edge (the SIUS trio merges into one baby wearing a hat), leaving a small blinking circle. Tap the circle, or swipe inward from it, to bring the buttons back (they follow your finger). Hold any button and drag (or fling) it to the left, right, top or bottom to tuck the buttons on that side; the small “⌂” button at the bottom left of the group returns them to the default position. To stop auto-tucking, turn it off in “Settings › Interface”.</li><li><b>Call·Check</b>: after each shot, tap where you felt it went on the target. Hold to drag and fine-tune; it's recorded when you release. Pinch to zoom (tap the top right to return to the full target). The top left shows the latest shot's score; the bottom right has “New set / Clear set / Undo”. Scroll down after shooting for <b>Check</b>.</li><li><b>Check</b> (below the calls): bring the target back and take a photo; the app <b>finds the black to calibrate and finds the shot holes automatically</b>. The green circles should match the rings; the purple dashed lines should pass through the ring numbers on all four sides; if tilted, tap “Rotate”. Tap a wrongly found hole to delete it, tap empty space to add a missed one. If auto calibration fails, tap “Manual calibration” (tap the center + the top, right, bottom and left of a ring). For electronic targets you can choose “Tap directly on the target”.</li><li><b>Analysis</b>: no calls needed. Take a photo after each target (any number of shots); the app calibrates, finds the holes and calculates stats for each target and combined: sight correction (group center offset in mm and correction direction), consistency (std dev, average without the worst shot), outliers, trend chart and decimal score distribution. Uncertain holes are marked with an orange dashed circle + “?”; tap to delete, and tap empty space to add missed ones (on the photo or the target below). Analyses are saved to “Records” automatically and can be renamed. “Copy record code” packs the hole coordinates into a string only this app can read; paste it into a notes app or LINE as a backup, and “Paste to restore” draws it back on the target later (photos not included).</li><li><b>Press crosshair</b>: when you hold any button, info card or list, a crosshair (same as for calls) appears where you press and disappears when you release; it doesn't affect taps or scrolling.</li><li><b>Compare records</b>: in “Records”, select 2 or more (analysis or calls) and tap “Compare” to see average, group size, offset, std dev, inner 10s and trends across dates.</li><li>A photo can't show shot order, so by default calls are matched by “nearest match” (one-to-one with the shortest total distance); if you tap shots on the target in order, you can switch to “by shot order”. The app calculates the difference for each call, the average error and your systematic call bias.</li><li><b>Records</b>: each set is saved automatically on this device.</li><li><b>Export PDF</b>: “Export PDF” on the Analysis page, or “PDF” on any record, makes an A4 report (overview, analysis, each target; optional shot list and 1:1 actual-size target; records with calls also get call-vs-actual pages). Pick one of 10 themes and Traditional Chinese or English. It is made on your phone and never uploaded.</li></ol><h3>Target dimensions (outer diameter, mm)</h3><p>Source: ISSF Rule Book 2026 Edition 2025 (Third Print 10/2026, effective 2026-10-19). The targets are drawn as vectors from these dimensions.</p><table><tr><th colspan=\"3\">10m Air Pistol (6.3.4.6)</th></tr><tr><th>Ring</th><th>Outer diameter</th><th>Scoring limit (center distance)</th></tr>{3}</table><p>Inner ten 5.0mm; black = rings 7–10 = 59.5mm; numbers 1–8.</p><table><tr><th colspan=\"3\">10m Air Rifle (6.3.4.3)</th></tr><tr><th>Ring</th><th>Outer diameter</th><th>Scoring limit (center distance)</th></tr>{4}</table><p>The 10 ring is a 0.5mm white dot; black = rings 4–9 = 30.5mm; inner ten = the white 10 dot completely shot out; numbers 1–8.</p><h3>Scoring</h3><p>Shot holes are taken as 4.5mm in diameter (air gun caliber, 7.4.6 / 8.4.3.5). A hole that touches the outer edge of a higher ring scores the higher value, so a “center distance ≤ ring outer radius + 2.25mm” scores that ring. Decimal scores follow 6.3.3.1, dividing each ring into ten equal parts: decimal score = 10 + (10-ring limit − center distance) ÷ ring width (pistol 8mm, rifle 2.5mm), truncated to 0.1, maximum 10.9. Inner ten: pistol = the hole touches the 5.0mm inner ten ring (center distance ≤ 4.75mm); rifle = the 0.5mm white dot is completely inside the hole (center distance ≤ 2.0mm). These are ideal geometric calculations; official scoring is decided by the judges' scoring gauges and electronic targets.</p><h3>Beta limitations</h3><ul><li>Automatic hole detection: tightly overlapping groups may be under- or over-counted, so check against your number of calls. Holes outside the black (on white paper) have lower contrast and are missed more easily.</li><li>Photo rotation can't be detected automatically; the top of the photo is assumed to be the top of the target. If tilted, use “Rotate”.</li><li>Calibration uses an ellipse + affine transform; a photo taken at a steep angle (perspective distortion) can shift the center by about 0.5–1mm, so shoot as straight on as possible.</li><li>Photos aren't stored; only the converted coordinates are saved.</li></ul>",
