  "mods.what_is": "What is {0}?",
  "mods.close": "Close",
  "mods.n": "Number of shot holes counted in the current view (one target or combined).",
  "mods.total": "Sum of the integer ring scores. The small number is the decimal total (ISSF 6.3.3.1 decimal scoring, max 10.9 per shot).",
  "mods.avg": "Total ÷ number of shots. The small number is the decimal average.",
  "mods.p60": "Current average × 60, converted to a 60-shot total. It is a conversion, not a prediction.",
  "mods.inner": "Air pistol: the hole touches the 5.0 mm inner ten ring. Air rifle: the white 10 dot is completely shot out (ISSF 6.3.4).",
  "mods.offset": "Direction of the mean point of impact (average of all hole positions) from the centre; the small text is the average distance from the centre.",
  "mods.group": "Distance between the two shots farthest apart, centre to centre (mm).",
  "mods.rings": "How many shots scored 10, 9, 8, 7 and below 7 (integer rings).",
  "mods.series": "Integer and decimal totals for every 10 shots, in shooting order.",
  "mods.adv": "Where the group centre is (after excluding outliers), how many mm from the centre, and which way the shots should move. Converting to sight clicks depends on the gun, so WRbeta does not do it yet. WRbeta practice reference, not an ISSF rule.",
  "mods.stab": "Standard deviation of the decimal scores shows how spread out the scores are; smaller is steadier. The average without the worst shot shows your level without the one bad shot.",
  "mods.out": "Shots unusually far from the group centre (median position). A shot is flagged when its distance exceeds the largest of median + 3 × robust SD, 2 × median, and half a ring width; needs at least 5 shots. WRbeta statistical reference, not an ISSF rule.",
  "mods.trend": "Average decimal score per target or per 10 shots, with a least-squares trend line; a change under 0.15 counts as flat.",
  "mods.hist": "One bar per 0.1 from 10.0 to 10.9; scores below 10 are grouped, so you can see where your scores cluster.",
  "mods.sheets": "Shots, total, decimal total, average, group size and uncertain holes for each target. Tap a row to view just that target.",
  "mods.list": "Ring, decimal score, inner ten, X/Y position and distance from the centre for every shot (mm; right and up are positive).",
  "mods.heat": "Keeps the group's shape and slides the whole group to every position on the target to compute the decimal total; warmer colours mean higher scores. Lost to group centre offset = points you would gain by moving the group to the best position. Lost to group size = points still missing from 10.9 per shot at the best position because the shots are spread. The sight advice moves the group centre to the best position. WRbeta practice reference, not an ISSF rule.",
  "mods.teiler": "Teiler = distance from the hole centre to the target centre in 1/100 mm; smaller is better (e.g. 125 = 1.25 mm). It is common in German shooting and on electronic targets but is not an ISSF rule term. WRbeta uses plain 1/100 mm for both pistol and rifle, with no conversion factor.",
  "mods.dir": "Direction of each shot from the centre (arrow) and clock position (12 = straight up, 3 = right), with its decimal score. Shots very close to the centre are marked centred.",
  "mods.spread": "Mean radius circle: centred on the mean point of impact, radius = average distance of the shots from it. Minimum enclosing circle: the smallest circle containing every hole centre. Horizontal / vertical spread = left-most to right-most and lowest to highest (centre to centre).",
  "mods.callact": "Each arrow runs from your call to the matched actual hole; shorter arrows mean better calls. Matching is the same as in the comparison.",
  "mods.centers": "Blue cross = average position of your calls; red cross = average position of the matched actual holes. The gap shows whether your feel is consistently off in one direction.",
  "mods.calltrend": "Call error (mm) for each shot in call order, with a least-squares trend line, to see whether your calls get better or worse; a change under 0.5 mm counts as flat.",
